.SH "NAME"
.PP
xrelay \- relay daemon: SMTP, MQTT, FTP and syslog proxies
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBxrelay\fR [\fB\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-validate\fR] [\fB\-version\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxrelay\fR is the relay: the daemon machines talk to. It reads one YAML configuration file (see \fBxproxy.yaml\fR(5)), binds the listeners it describes or takes them from systemd socket activation, and serves until stopped.
.PP
It serves the listener kinds of the relay role: \fBsmtp\fR (mail and submission, with STARTTLS), \fBmqtt\fR (3.1.1 and 5.0), \fBftp\fR and \fBsyslog\fR. There are no interactive sessions here and no recordings; the traffic comes from devices and services, and the policy is written in each protocol\(aqs own terms \[u2014] which commands, topics, facilities and paths are allowed, and what the relay does with a message it will not pass.
.PP
The internet\-facing HTTP machinery its sibling \fBxproxy\fR(8) runs, and the interactive access its sibling \fBxgate\fR(8) mediates, are separate programs and separate processes with separate users. A listener of another role in the same file is validated in full and left to its owner, which is what lets the three share a set of includes; a listener kind this binary did not link is never bound.
.PP
The process runs as the \fBxrelay\fR user under the shipped systemd unit and confines itself with Landlock, seccomp and a dropped capability set (see \fBdocs/HARDENING.md\fR). It is controlled at run time through its own management socket by \fBxproxyctl\fR(8).
